        {
          "id": 897462469,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "The guideline is that [PI_MALFORMED is for impossible values that cause dissection to be aborted, whereas PI_PROTOCOL is for a violation of the specfication but dissection is probably continued.](https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChAdvExpert.html)\n\nExpert errors do not always follow the guidelines perfectly - for example, the opensafety CRC errors should be [PI_CHECKSUM and PI_WARN](epan/proto.h#L910-911). Some of those BER ones perhaps should also be PI_PROTOCOL - there's a BER option to decode unexpected ASN.1 objections. PI_PROTOCOL is usually PI_WARN and PI_MALFORMED is usually PI_ERROR, but there are exceptions, possibly out of some thought that certain errors are impossible to recover from but somehow common and not \"severe\" enough to be top priority.\n\nUsing the existing `ei_ber_error_length` would be fine, I think. It's typically used with `proto_tree_add_expert_format` - there's a couple functions like `ber_add_bad_length_error` and `ber_add_large_length_error` (the latter specifically for length longer than the tvb remaining but we don't want to throw an exception I reckon because we don't want to abort dissection.)\n\nMost important to me is that using that length value doesn't necessarily seem like the sort of thing that should throw an exception and abort processing.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
